-- Same 36-Field board as valid-instance.soil, EXCEPT Field6 (row.index=1,
-- column.index=6 -- NOT one of the 10 given-clue cells) has value 7
-- instead of 1.
--
-- Why an out-of-range value, not an in-range duplicate: the task template
-- for this fixture (see GraphColoring's invalid-instance.soil) suggests
-- "duplicate a value within one row/column/box" as the minimal violation.
-- That does NOT work here, and trying it is itself a finding worth
-- recording. In a fully-solved 6x6 Sudoku grid, every row, column, and
-- 2x3 box is independently a permutation of 1..6 (isUnique over exactly 6
-- Fields with allowedValue restricting to 6 possible values forces this).
-- Changing any single Field to a DIFFERENT in-range value (1..6) is
-- therefore never a single-invariant violation: by the pigeonhole
-- principle, the other 5 cells of that field's row already cover the
-- other 5 values, and likewise for its column and its 2x3 square, so the
-- new value necessarily collides with the row AND the column AND the
-- square simultaneously (three invariants at once), or a fourth
-- (givenRxCy) as well if the chosen cell happens to be one of the 10
-- given clues. This was confirmed directly: an earlier draft of this file
-- set Field2 (row1,col2, not given) from 3 to 2 (matching Field4 in the
-- same row) and running `check -v` against it reported 3 failures
-- (uniqueValuesRow, uniqueValuesColumn, uniqueValuesSquare all at once),
-- not 1 -- confirming the pigeonhole argument empirically, not just in
-- theory.
--
-- The actual minimal single-invariant violation used below instead: set
-- Field6.value to 7, outside the 1..6 range allowedValue requires. Since
-- 7 does not already appear anywhere in Field6's row, column, or 2x3
-- square (it's a value genuinely foreign to that whole 6x6 board, not one
-- of the 6 values already in play there), isUnique still holds everywhere
-- -- uniqueValuesRow/Column/Square, and every one of the 10 given-clue
-- invariants, are all completely unaffected. Only allowedValue fails, and
-- only for Field6. Confirmed by actually running this (see
-- invalid-instance.cmd): `check -v` reports allowedValue violated for
-- Field6 alone and every other invariant OK, finishing with "checked 18
-- invariants ..., 1 failure." (USE checks a class invariant as one
-- Field.allInstances->forAll statement, so this counts as ONE failure,
-- not one per violating object -- consistent with how GraphColoring's own
-- invalid-instance.cmd comment documents the same counting rule).

